nes just  dangling there like Lleida herself in  the indifferent beak of the Swan in this  way Yeats commits a kind of violence on  the sonnet form not for nothing the poet  is attempting to augment the violent  content of his work sonnets at least she  experienced on its are traditionally  about great love and yet here Yeats uses  the form to describe what could be  considered the exact opposite an act of  violent rape and if you look at  successive versions of Lleida and the  Swan you can see that Yeats  turned up the volume on this violence  for example in one verse  the poem begins a rush a sudden wheel  and hovering still the bird descends her  frail thighs pressed by the webbed toes  and that all-powerful bill has laid her  helpless face upon his breast now the  early version almost assumes ladies  acquiescence to the gods advances in the  later version beginning with the shock  of a sudden blow Zeus's molestation is  intensely emphasized and emphasized - is  Lleida and the readers disorientation  the great wings beating still the  ambiguity of beating still how can wings  be still if they're beating and on the  other hand if he means that they're  still beating  that means terrifyingly that the rape  has already begun while the Swan is  still in the air now in this version the  girl is staggering her thighs caressed a  word that disturbs us by commingling the  erotic with the vicious something that  Yeats does a bunch in this poem caressed  by the dark webs that gives a sense of  just how big the bird is because not  even the toes but the webs between the  toes are what's caressing her and it  should be noted that both the first and  second line end with enjambment pulling  us awkwardly through the verse and it's  not until the last line of that first  that we finally get the subject he as if  everything was happening so fast that  Lleida couldn't find her bearings until  the gruesome act was already underway  the first quatrain of ley de and the  Swan is dis